export const initialState={powerRole:'bridge'};
const scene=(id,label,title,reference,explanation,sources,extra={})=>({id,label,title,reference,objective:'integration',pedagogical_role:'case-study',explanation:[explanation],sources,...extra});
const S=finaleSources;
export const scenes=[
 scene('abilene-factory','Abilene','Putting an AI Factory Together','d15-upgrade-and-evidence','Return to the original Oracle/OpenAI campus. The closing chapter follows one real facility through workload requirements, supply, heat rejection, delivery and finance. The adjacent Microsoft project is a separate development. The photograph is Oracle’s July 15, 2026 aerial; it does not establish how many depicted halls were operating.',[S.campus],{pedagogical_role:'opening'}),
 scene('abilene-workload','Start with the workload','Start with the hardware, then size the facility.','d02-workload-brief','OpenAI reported the first GB200 racks arriving in June 2025 and early training and inference by July. Connect this real workload to three interfaces: rack power, liquid and residual-air heat capture, and the fabric that connects racks. Work backward from processor demand through conversion losses, other rack loads and supporting infrastructure. The Chapter 8 example starts with 72 kW at the processor rails, not total rack demand: it needs 90.26 kW on the rack DC bus and 93.05 kW at the AC inlet. Those are illustrative values, not Abilene rack ratings. Residual air heat means heat not captured by the cold plates; an RDHX or room-air system may capture it, but the public evidence does not establish every Abilene hall’s arrangement. These are the same interfaces taught earlier, now requirements on a real campus. Do not infer tokens per second, a particular model, GPU inventory or utilization from the campus rating.',[S.workloads,S.live]),
 scene('abilene-power','Bridge power becomes backup','Abilene’s gas plant has a role before and after grid expansion.','d03-power-and-procurement','Crusoe’s 2025 Impact Report describes 350 MW of on-site gas generation as temporary bridge power and long-term backup. This is an intentional synthesis of Chapter 4: connect the plant’s role to the campus delivery schedule, then to continuity. Toggle the plant’s role while keeping the same equipment. Bridge generation can advance delivery, but requires fuel infrastructure, emissions controls and operation; the equipment then retains a continuity role. This conceptual supply diagram does not specify installed switchgear, source proportions or transfer timing. The gas rating does not establish backup for the full planned campus.',[S.impact],{controls:[{key:'powerRole',label:'Gas plant role',options:[['bridge','Bridge power'],['backup','Long-term backup']]}]}),
 scene('abilene-cooling-choice','Choose outdoor cooling','How could this campus reject heat?','d11-heat-rejection','Compare three heat-rejection routes already taught: direct dry cooling, an air-cooled chiller, and a water-cooled chiller with a wet tower. Direct dry cooling depends on sufficiently cool outdoor air relative to the required water temperature; refrigeration can supply colder water when that temperature gap is unavailable. The highlighted air-cooled chiller is the published Abilene choice because its outdoor rejection avoids evaporating water. These are architectural alternatives, not a claim about Crusoe’s actual procurement shortlist. Air here describes outdoor heat rejection, not air-cooled GPUs.',[S.design,S.chiller]),
 scene('abilene-heat','Water shapes the cooling','Abilene chose air-cooled chillers to conserve water.','d11-heat-rejection','Crusoe describes a recirculating facility-water loop with air-cooled chillers, accepting higher lifecycle and maintenance costs for water conservation. Follow heat across the rack-side and facility-side interfaces to the refrigerant condenser and outdoor air. The diagram summarizes those functions without claiming an as-built pipe arrangement or a particular residual-air device. A closed facility loop still needs initial fill and maintenance water.',[S.design]),
 scene('abilene-parallel-build','Build in parallel','Crusoe built electrical equipment while the campus took shape.','d13-delivery-dependencies','Crusoe reports in-house switchgear manufacture and prefabricated electrical skids for Abilene. Its September 14, 2026 release reports more than 2,500 switchboards supplied from Tulsa to Abilene. The September 2026 assembly-floor photograph is a current manufacturing example; it is not evidence that the newly opened second factory supplied the first 2025 phase. The two photographs show real Crusoe electrical manufacturing and actual Abilene construction, converging at installation and testing without fabricated lead times. A manufacturing photograph does not establish the identity of the particular package installed in a specific Abilene hall. Preserve the valuable lesson from the former retrofit case: the package must meet electrical interfaces and remain accessible for installation and maintenance. Factory completion alone does not establish an operating hall.',[S.impact,S.manufacturing]),
 scene('abilene-capital','Finance the physical campus','Long-term demand supports investment in the buildings.','d15-cost-per-service','The initial project was fully leased long-term to an unnamed hyperscaler in the October 2024 financing announcement. In May 2025 Crusoe, Blue Owl and Primary Digital announced the second phase of a $15 billion joint venture for the campus. Treat the venture as facility funding: it is not a disclosed GPU purchase bill, cloud revenue or profit. Crusoe is a participant in the joint venture funding the campus; the first-phase source describes the facility as leased to a hyperscaler. It does not establish that Crusoe leases the building from another company to eliminate its liability. The diagram connects the facility investment to the customer’s capacity commitment without inventing loan terms or the Oracle/OpenAI billing rate.',[S.capital,S.lease]),
 scene('abilene-live-expansion','Grow around live workloads','Abilene ran its first workloads while the campus kept growing.','d13-commissioning-complete-paths','The first two buildings were energized within a year of the June 2024 construction start; the project continued toward eight buildings and an integrated network fabric. The drawing preserves the distinction between a working phase and construction. Use it to discuss the interfaces of the next phase: electrical connection, heat rejection and network change. Keeping them isolated until ready is the engineering implication, not a claim about a published Abilene commissioning procedure or topology. This replaces the old acceptance-flag quiz and invented six-/seven-/twelve-day schedule.',[S.live,S.workloads]),
 scene('abilene-system','The whole machine','The data center is the machine.','d15-upgrade-and-evidence','Zoom out from individual equipment to the three physical scales: campus, data hall and rack. At Abilene the customer commitment supports investment; the GB200 workload sets rack interfaces; water constraints influence heat rejection; manufacturing and bridge power affect delivery; and phased expansion has to preserve the operating service. Use the campus and hall photographs plus the manufacturer rack image as the visual recap rather than re-teaching equations or another equipment flowchart. Installed capacity alone never specifies a token rate.',[S.campus,S.workloads,S.live],{pedagogical_role:'synthesis'}),
 scene('watts-to-work','From Watts to Tokens','From Watts to Tokens','d15-upgrade-and-evidence','End inside the actual Abilene data hall after the preceding campus-to-rack recap. Suggested closing line: A token depends on much more than a chip. You can now follow its physical requirements back through the rack, the cooling plant, the electrical supply, and the decisions that made the campus possible. The full-frame Oracle photograph is real; the separately supplied ChatGPT image prompt offers a future conceptual closing illustration, not evidence of Abilene’s as-built design.',[S.workloads],{pedagogical_role:'closing'})
];
